    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d37">

                <pb facs="0133" n="31" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg31"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A4 General Committee (Arms)/118:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        American Delegate</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Wilson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-03-07T19:00:00-05:00">March 7, 1935—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>482. Your Nos. 999, March 5, 11 a.m.,<anchor n="55" xml:id="d37-f1"/> and 1000
                    March 5, 7 p.m. No objection is perceived to the transfer of Category IV (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">a</hi>) (1) to Category I.</p>
                <pb facs="0134" n="32" xml:id="pg32"/>
                <p>The transfer of Category V (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) (2) to Category I
                    would appear to be inexpedient and unnecessary. Our manufacturers and those of
                    other producing countries have a legitimate export trade in revolvers and
                    pistols to meet the requirements of civilians for individual protection.
                    Although these articles are an element of military equipment, they are primarily
                    and for the most part used for other than military purposes. It would,
                    therefore, appear to be inadmissible to prohibit their exportation except “for
                    direct supply to the government of the importing state or, with the consent of
                    such government, to a public authority subordinate to it” as provided for in
                    Article 10.</p>
                <p>We would have no objection to the elimination of Category V (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">a</hi>) (1) and Category V (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >a</hi>) (3).</p>
                <p>Your position in regard to Articles 14 and 18 of the Washington Naval Treaty is
                    approved.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
